From 6c1b8a3c14538908da1508c97d629d661982dbd2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alex Booster <webdevbooster@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 17:29:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Tell the inner Rich a stored entry can be out of date,
 not just missing

The standing instruction had one half of the record's honesty and not the
other. It said the record is partial and that an absent entry proves
nothing - and then, in the same breath, that "an entry in it is proof the
thing happened", with no clause anywhere saying an entry describes the
moment it was written rather than now. A grep for obsolete|stale|no longer
describes|superseded|out of date over the 63-line file returned nothing
(exit 1).

That is not a theoretical gap. On the operator side, on one day: two items
in a person's own list said "do this" for work already finished and sat
that way for 48 hours; a record row claimed five test suites were red when
none of them were; and two open escalations rested on premises that had
since gone false, one of them naming a workflow as disabled while it was
active. A Rich reading any of those would have believed them, because this
file told it to, and handed a confident wrong answer to the person it works
for.

Two ideas, in the file's own register (design section 4.2 item 3, extended
from "how absence is reported" to how absence AND staleness are reported -
both halves are invariant, so neither is the conditional kind 4.3 excludes):

  - An entry is proof the thing happened; it is not proof the thing is
    still true. When an answer rests on a stored entry and it would change
    what he does next, look at the thing itself first, and say so when you
    have not.
  - "This is broken" and "this description is old" are two different
    findings, and working out which one it is is not his job. Say which,
    or say that you cannot tell yet and what would settle it.

No machinery, no lists, no incident, no example - 4.3's exclusions bind
this addition as much as the rest of the file.

  63 lines -> 74 lines, 3206 -> 3769 bytes.
  Worst case render (32-char name, per the budget test) = 139 provenance
  + 3769 - 17 placeholder + 47 name line = 3938 bytes against 4.3's 4096.
  158 bytes of headroom left, which is the tightest this file has been.

action_ledger_tests.rs::the_ledgers_partial_coverage_is_stated_rather_than_overclaimed
pins that sentence verbatim, line breaks included, and it is the test that
deliberately followed the guarantee when it moved channel on 2026-09-06.
Its pins move with the sentence rather than around it: the asymmetry it was
written for is still asserted, and the missing half is now asserted beside
it.

  cargo test -p richos-core: 1035 passed, 0 failed, 4 ignored
  (1030 direct + 4 ignored + 5 doc-tests; the README's "1034" is the static
  count of #[test] across src/ and tests/, which includes the 4 ignored).
